<article class="env-remark" id="07IM">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07IM">Remark <span data-tag="07IM">60.9.6</span> <span class="named">(Structure morphism)</span>.</a>  In Situation <a href="/tag/07MF" data-tag="07MF">60.7.5</a>. Consider the closed subscheme $S_0 = V(\mathcal{I}) \subset S$. If we assume that $p$ is locally nilpotent on $S_0$ (which is always the case in practice) then we obtain a situation as in Definition <a href="/tag/07I6" data-tag="07I6">60.8.1</a> with $S_0$ instead of $X$. Hence we get a site $\text{Cris}(S_0/S)$. If $f : X \to S_0$ is the structure morphism of $X$ over $S$, then we get a commutative diagram of topoi </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ (X/S)_{\text{cris}} \ar[r]_{f_{\text{cris}}} \ar[d]_{u_{X/S}} &  (S_0/S)_{\text{cris}} \ar[d]^{u_{S_0/S}} \\  \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (X_{Zar}) \ar[r]^{f_{small}} &  \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (S_{0, Zar}) \ar[rd] \\  &  &  \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (S_{Zar}) }  \]
</div>
  <p> see Remark <a href="/tag/07IK" data-tag="07IK">60.9.3</a>. We think of the composition $(X/S)_{\text{cris}} \to \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (S_{Zar})$ as the structure morphism of the crystalline site. Even if $p$ is not locally nilpotent on $S_0$ the structure morphism </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \tau _{X/S} : (X/S)_{\text{cris}} \longrightarrow \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (S_{Zar})  \]
</div>
  <p> is defined as we can take the lower route through the diagram above. </p>
</article>